## Claim
Text: "Previous flu vaccines may have made President Donald Trump susceptible to COVID-19"
Claim author: Miriam Fauzia
Claim date: October 21, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Did President Donald Trump receive any influenza vaccines in the years prior to the COVID-19 pandemic?
Answer: Based on the source, President Donald Trump received three flu shots in the years prior to his COVID-19 diagnosis, as the article references "the president's flu vaccination history" and a clip from an interview "that refers to the president's flu vaccination history," noting that the Instagram post in question asks, "Did Trump's previous three flu vaccines make him more susceptible to contracting CVD?"

Source URL: https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/10/21/fact-check-donald-trumps-flu-shots-covid-risk-arent-linked/3623699001/

### Is there any peer-reviewed scientific study that demonstrates a causal link between prior influenza vaccination and increased susceptibility to COVID-19?
Answer: No, the peer-reviewed systematic review and meta-analysis found that influenza vaccination was associated with a reduced risk of COVID-19 infection (RR=0.83, 95% CI 0.76–0.90), not an increased susceptibility. No study in the search result supports a causal link to increased susceptibility.

Source URL: https://pesquisa.bvsalud.org/global-literature-on-novel-coronavirus-2019-ncov/resource/pt/covidwho-1889175

### Did President Donald Trump test positive for COVID-19 before or after the publication date of this claim (October 21, 2020)?
Answer: President Donald Trump announced his COVID-19 diagnosis on October 2, 2020, which is before the publication date of the claim on October 21, 2020. The search result confirms that “the president announced his COVID-19 diagnosis in the early morning of Oct. 2.”

Source URL: https://www.lawfaremedia.org/article/public-disclosure-presidential-illnesses-discouraging-history

### What is the biological mechanism by which prior flu vaccination could increase susceptibility to SARS-CoV-2 infection?
Answer: The search result does not describe a specific biological mechanism by which prior flu vaccination could increase susceptibility to SARS-CoV-2 infection. It only mentions a theoretical concern about "vaccine-induced immune enhancement" and notes that "non-neutralizing antibodies following influenza vaccination or infection have amplified disease severity following a heterologous influenza challenge," but it does not connect this to SARS-CoV-2 or explain how prior flu vaccination could increase susceptibility. Additionally, the study's own adjusted analysis finds that influenza vaccination does not increase COVID-19 incidence or severity. Therefore, the provided source does not contain sufficient information to answer the question.

Source URL: https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-clinical-and-translational-science/article/safety-of-influenza-vaccine-during-covid19/C26A417046677E65D421AD33B3E6281F

### Have major health organizations, such as the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) or the World Health Organization (WHO), issued any statements supporting or refuting the claim that flu vaccines increase COVID-19 risk?
Answer: Based on the provided search result, there is no direct statement from major health organizations such as the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) or the World Health Organization (WHO) explicitly supporting or refuting the claim that flu vaccines increase COVID-19 risk. The article mentions a study from the Journal of Clinical and Translational Science that found no increased risk, but it does not cite an official statement from these organizations on the matter.

Source URL: https://www.medicalnewstoday.com/articles/flu-vaccinations-not-linked-to-increased-covid-19-risk

### Are there any known epidemiological studies that show a correlation between prior flu vaccination and COVID-19 incidence in the general population?
Answer: Yes, a systematic review and meta-analysis of 23 studies involving over 1 million participants found that prior influenza vaccination was associated with a reduced risk of COVID-19 infection (RR=0.83, 95% CI=0.76, 0.90), indicating a protective correlation rather than increased susceptibility.

Source URL: https://pesquisa.bvsalud.org/global-literature-on-novel-coronavirus-2019-ncov/resource/pt/covidwho-1889175

### Could confounding factors (such as age, underlying health conditions, or exposure to SARS-CoV-2) account for any observed association between flu vaccination and COVID-19 susceptibility?
Answer: Yes, the study accounted for confounding factors such as age, body mass index, comorbidities, and other clinical characteristics using overlap propensity score weighting, and after adjustment, found that influenza vaccination was unrelated to the incidence of SARS-CoV-2 infection (adjusted OR 0.79 [0.62–1.00]) and did not worsen COVID-19 outcomes, indicating that any observed unadjusted associations were likely due to these confounders.

Source URL: https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-clinical-and-translational-science/article/safety-of-influenza-vaccine-during-covid19/C26A417046677E65D421AD33B3E6281F

## Final Judgement
### Step 1: Summary of Key Insights
The fact-check examined the claim that previous flu vaccines may have made President Donald Trump susceptible to COVID-19. Evidence shows that Trump had received three flu shots prior to his COVID-19 diagnosis. However, peer-reviewed scientific studies—including a systematic review and meta-analysis of over one million participants—found that influenza vaccination is associated with a *reduced* risk of COVID-19 infection (RR=0.83), not an increased risk. No biological mechanism linking prior flu vaccination to increased SARS-CoV-2 susceptibility was established, and adjusted analyses accounting for confounders (age, health conditions, etc.) showed no increased incidence or severity of COVID-19. Major health organizations have not supported the claim, and available epidemiological evidence contradicts it. The evidence is clear and consistent, requiring no further investigation.

### Step 3: Conclusion
The claim that previous flu vaccines may have made President Donald Trump susceptible to COVID-19 is not supported by any credible evidence. On the contrary, the available scientific studies demonstrate that influenza vaccination is associated with a lower risk of COVID-19 infection and does not increase susceptibility. Therefore, the claim is refuted.

